    {
      "id": 8,
      "type": "core.input.comment",
      "name": "comment",
      "x": 56,
      "y": 17.9,
      "text": "The \"sample-and-hold\" circuit is a classic synthesis technique.\n\nThe \"impulse\" is controlling a latch, which grabs, or samples, the current value from the sawOsc.\nAt the same time it triggers the envelope.\nThe sampled value is used to control the frequency of the sineOsc.\n\nToDo:\nIncrease the impulse frequency to the maximum and reduce the sawOsc frequency to the <1.0.\nSlowly change those values until their relation is clear.\nThen slow down the impulse freq to around 5 and increase the swaOsc frequency to around 14.\nThe odd patterns that emerge is because we are often under-sampling the sawOsc and causing aliasing.",
      "w": 765.75,
      "h": 212
    },
